claim:
needs: 2
title: Claude Code integration: a skill and permissions, not an MCP server
effort: M
area: cli

Agents reach pidly through Bash today, with only the AGENTS.md block telling
them it exists. That works and is the whole point of the format, but nothing
makes an agent claim before starting, release when it stops, or stop asking
for permission on every pidly call. This is the packaging for that.

Three surfaces exist and they are not the same thing:

- A tool is a function the model calls with typed arguments. The built-ins
  are Bash, Read, Edit and friends; the only way to add one is an MCP
  server, a process the client talks to over stdio or http.
- A skill is instructions, loaded when relevant, that the model acts on
  using the tools it already has — so pidly commands run through Bash.
- Hooks and settings.json permissions are the glue: run something at session
  start or stop, pre-approve Bash(pidly list:*) so the prompts stop.

Decision: a skill plus commands plus permissions. Not an MCP server.

An MCP server is a running process, and the format's premise is that there
is no server and nothing to install — a project that copies .pidly/ as a
folder gets the whole tracker, and an agent in a checkout where pidly was
never installed still works with find, sed and mv. Wrapping a CLI that Bash
already reaches would add a process and a schema for no new capability.

What tools would genuinely buy, for when this is worth revisiting: typed
arguments, so a malformed invocation is rejected before it runs; structured
results instead of text the model has to parse; and per-tool permission
gating, so `reap` and `close` can be prompted while `list` is not. Reopen
this decision if agents are getting pidly's arguments wrong in practice, or
if something starts consuming pidly's output as data rather than reading it.

Scope:

- A skill that tells an agent the loop — claim before starting, release with
  -m when stopping early, close when done, never delete — and points at
  .pidly/README.md rather than restating it. The AGENTS.md block is the
  format; the skill is the workflow.
- Slash commands for the things worth one keystroke: what can I start, what
  am I holding, hand this back.
- A permissions block for settings.json: the read-only commands (list, tree,
  status, check) allowed outright, the writing ones left to prompt.
- Optionally a session hook: dead claims and held-but-idle tickets surfaced
  at the start of a session, or a warning at the end about claims this
  session still holds. Decide whether that is a hook or just advice in the
  skill — a hook is enforcement, and enforcement in this project has so far
  been the operator's job.

Open questions:

- Does pidly generate these files the way it generates AGENTS.md and
  .pidly/README.md, and does check compare them against what would be
  generated now? Same staleness argument applies if so.
- Does that go in `pidly agents`, which already owns "tell the agents", or a
  separate command? `agents -f` already takes a target file, and a skill is
  a directory, not a file.
- Other clients exist. Cursor, Gemini and Codex have their own formats;
  `pidly agents` already writes to whichever of their files it finds. Decide
  whether this ticket is Claude-only or the first of several.

Done when a fresh checkout can be handed to Claude Code and the agent
claims, works and closes without being told how in the prompt.
